= Entity types =

'''An entity type describes what kind of knowledge object a page principally represents.''' It helps readers and queries distinguish a method from a publication, a project from an implementation, and an abstract concept from a specific technological system.

Types are chosen to support clear knowledge representation. They are not exhaustive claims about what exists in nature, and they should not force complex subjects into misleading categories.

== Current types ==

The following twelve values form the controlled entity-type vocabulary used by BITwiki's knowledge-object validator.

{| class="wikitable"
! Type !! Meaning !! Illustrative subject
|-
| '''Concept''' || An idea, phenomenon, principle, theory, model or conceptual distinction studied or explained as a subject. || Feedback, causality, photosynthesis
|-
| '''Method''' || A systematic approach to investigation, calculation, analysis, design or practice. || Sensitivity analysis, finite-element analysis
|-
| '''Protocol''' || A prescribed procedure, agreement or specification governing actions or exchanges. || Communication protocol, experimental procedure
|-
| '''Implementation''' || A particular realization of a design, method, concept or protocol in software, hardware or practice. || A named software client implementing a protocol
|-
| '''Project''' || An organized undertaking with goals, participants, work and outputs. || A defined research or engineering initiative
|-
| '''Dataset''' || An identifiable collection of data organized for analysis, distribution, validation or reuse. || A released genomic sequence dataset
|-
| '''Person''' || An individual represented as the subject of biography, attribution or participation. || A researcher or historical contributor
|-
| '''Organization''' || An institution, group, company, community or structured collective. || A laboratory, standards body or research consortium
|-
| '''Event''' || A bounded occurrence or period whose participants, time, setting or consequences matter. || A research expedition or scientific conference
|-
| '''Publication''' || A specific work made available to an audience, such as a paper, book, report or specification. || A scientific article or technical report
|-
| '''Location''' || A place or spatially meaningful environment treated as a subject in its own right. || A research station or geographic region
|-
| '''Technology''' || An engineered tool, system, platform or technological capability considered as a subject. || A sensor, processor architecture or software platform
|}

Examples illustrate the meaning of each type; they are not assertions that those particular pages exist or already carry those annotations.

== How to choose a primary type ==

Start with the question '''what does this page actually explain?'''

# Identify the subject and the purpose of the article.
# Consider the closest type in the current vocabulary.
# Check whether the type accurately distinguishes the subject from its neighboring concepts.
# Preserve other meaningful roles through the article and supported relationships, rather than guessing a new universal type.
# Revisit a classification when evidence, subject identity or a justified schema revision changes it.

A page about a method should not be classified as an Implementation solely because the method can be implemented. A publication that reports a result is not identical to the result it reports. An organization can operate a project, but the project and organization have different identities.

A general scientific subject does not require an information-processing mechanism in order to deserve a canonical article. Where a specialized type does not yet exist, <code>Concept</code> can describe the article's treatment of a scientific phenomenon without implying that the physical phenomenon is merely an abstract object.

== Important boundaries ==

=== Concept, Method, and Protocol ===

A '''Concept''' explains an idea or phenomenon. A '''Method''' describes an approach for achieving or investigating something. A '''Protocol''' specifies an expected sequence, set of rules or technical contract.

One field can contain all three. A statistical hypothesis is a concept; the procedure for testing it is a method; a detailed laboratory protocol specifies the required steps and conditions.

=== Technology and Implementation ===

A '''Technology''' describes an engineered capability or system. An '''Implementation''' emphasizes a particular realization of an existing design, specification, method or protocol.

A named client may be both technological and implement a network protocol. The page's central purpose determines its primary classification; the <code>Implements</code> relationship can describe the second role where supported.

=== Project and Organization ===

A '''Project''' is the undertaking; an '''Organization''' is the body or collective. Institutions may run multiple projects, and a project may involve several institutions. Membership and organizational responsibility should not be inferred from a shared name.

=== Publication and Dataset ===

A '''Publication''' is a documented communicative work; a '''Dataset''' is data prepared or identified for analysis and reuse. A publication may describe or use a dataset without being the dataset. Separate identities can improve attribution, version tracking and citation accuracy.

=== Person, Location, and Event ===

These represent human actors, places and occurrences as independent subjects when substantive treatment warrants it. A person's name or a place mentioned incidentally in another article does not require its own page. Type availability is not a notability, privacy or publication permission.

== Type is not domain or navigation ==

An article's '''entity type''' identifies its primary kind of subject. Its [[Property:Domain|domain]] identifies relevant fields of inquiry. Categories and portals help readers browse; they do not define the subject's nature.

For example, a Publication can have Biology and Medicine as subject domains while remaining a Publication. A portal for computational science can link Concepts, Datasets, Methods, People and Technologies without becoming the parent entity type of all of them.

[[BITwiki:Book Matter|Book Matter]] concerns composition and reuse within articles; it is not another automatic entity type.

== How the vocabulary is represented ==

<code>Property:Entity type</code> is a Semantic MediaWiki '''Text''' property. The controlled values are recorded in <code>bitwiki-runtime-schema.json</code> and mirrored in <code>Module:BITwiki/Data/Schema</code> for compiler-facing checks.

When a page invokes <code>Template:Knowledge object</code>, its <code>entity_type</code> parameter explicitly supplies the primary type. The template emits the relevant property assignment, and <code>Module:Structure</code> calls <code>Module:BITwiki/Compiler</code> to check required fields and supported values. An omitted field generates a diagnostic for that invocation; the template does not silently substitute <code>Concept</code>.

This describes the source-controlled template path. It does not imply that every page in the corpus has been annotated, that all readers use the template, or that live MediaWiki execution has been independently verified.

== Specialized types and schema evolution ==

Some disciplines may benefit from narrower types such as Species, Algorithm, Chemical substance, Biological process, Software or Instrument. These are '''possible refinements, not currently supported base values'''.

A proposed type should answer:

* Does it describe a stable distinction across multiple real knowledge objects?
* Is it different from a Domain, Category, relationship, or document section?
* Would it improve concrete queries, validation or reader understanding?
* Are its boundaries and overlaps with existing types explainable?
* Can existing data be reconciled without silently changing meaning?

Adding a type requires updating the controlled schema and its Lua projection, reviewing affected annotations, and validating the resulting queries and documentation. Until adopted, use the closest accurate existing type and explain any important nuance in prose.

Types may become more specialized, merge or be retired as understanding develops. Such changes should preserve provenance and avoid destroying meaningful distinctions merely to simplify a taxonomy.

